{"type":"message","message":{"id":"msg_9259bbe6-dd1e-4046-9122-38e695bf457c","role":"user","created":1775342427,"content":[{"type":"toolResponse","id":"call_7ab1fa30ab64452a9057f6e5","toolResult":{"status":"success","value":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"72:| Personal saving | Personal saving | 16 |\n73-| FEDERAL FISCAL OPERATIONS | FEDERAL FISCAL OPERATIONS | FEDERAL FISCAL OPERATIONS |\n74-| Analysis.--Budget results for the third quarter, fiscal 1991 | Analysis.--Budget results for the third quarter, fiscal 1991 | 19 |\n75-| FFO-1.--Summary of fiscal operations | FFO-1.--Summary of fiscal operations | 21 |\n76-| Chart.--Monthly receipts and outlays | Chart.--Monthly receipts and outlays | 22 |\n77-| FFO-2.--On-budget and off-budget receipts by source | FFO-2.--On-budget and off-budget receipts by source | 23 |\n--\n335:Consumer spending has begun to improve as employment opportunities increased in May and the moderation of inflation has helped to restore real purchasing power. Purchases of services such as medical care, personal care, transportation, household operations, education, personal business, and recreation have held up well even during the recession. Retail sales of durable and nondurable goods, including new cars, have strengthened following the disappointing results of last winter. Future gains are likely to be relatively modest, however, because of the low personal saving rate, continued consumer reluctance to add more debt, and the sluggish pace of prospective job and income gains.\n336-\n337-This article was prepared by the Assistant Secretary of the Treasury for Economic Policy for a special July 4, 1991, edition of The Japan Times.\n338-\n339-4\n340-\n--\n564:PERSONAL SAVING\n565-\n566:Household Saving as a Percent of After-Tax Income, Through First Half 1991\n567-\n568:The personal saving rate rose from a post-Depression low of 2.9 percent in 1987 to 4.6 percent in both 1989 and 1990, but remained well below the 6.7-percent long-term average. Saving appeared to be rising in early 1990, averaging 4.9 percent in the first half of the year. However, in the second half it dropped to only 4.2 percent as the slowing economy and increasing inflation reduced real incomes. The rate dipped to 3.7 percent in the second quarter of 1991, allowing only a 4-percent average for the first half of the year.\n569-\n570-17\n571-\n572-FEDERAL FISCAL OPERATIONS\n573-","annotations":{"priority":0.0}}],"structuredContent":{"stdout":"72:| Personal saving | Personal saving | 16 |\n73-| FEDERAL FISCAL OPERATIONS | FEDERAL FISCAL OPERATIONS | FEDERAL FISCAL OPERATIONS |\n74-| Analysis.--Budget results for the third quarter, fiscal 1991 | Analysis.--Budget results for the third quarter, fiscal 1991 | 19 |\n75-| FFO-1.--Summary of fiscal operations | FFO-1.--Summary of fiscal operations | 21 |\n76-| Chart.--Monthly receipts and outlays | Chart.--Monthly receipts and outlays | 22 |\n77-| FFO-2.--On-budget and off-budget receipts by source | FFO-2.--On-budget and off-budget receipts by source | 23 |\n--\n335:Consumer spending has begun to improve as employment opportunities increased in May and the moderation of inflation has helped to restore real purchasing power. Purchases of services such as medical care, personal care, transportation, household operations, education, personal business, and recreation have held up well even during the recession. Retail sales of durable and nondurable goods, including new cars, have strengthened following the disappointing results of last winter. Future gains are likely to be relatively modest, however, because of the low personal saving rate, continued consumer reluctance to add more debt, and the sluggish pace of prospective job and income gains.\n336-\n337-This article was prepared by the Assistant Secretary of the Treasury for Economic Policy for a special July 4, 1991, edition of The Japan Times.\n338-\n339-4\n340-\n--\n564:PERSONAL SAVING\n565-\n566:Household Saving as a Percent of After-Tax Income, Through First Half 1991\n567-\n568:The personal saving rate rose from a post-Depression low of 2.9 percent in 1987 to 4.6 percent in both 1989 and 1990, but remained well below the 6.7-percent long-term average. Saving appeared to be rising in early 1990, averaging 4.9 percent in the first half of the year. However, in the second half it dropped to only 4.2 percent as the slowing economy and increasing inflation reduced real incomes. The rate dipped to 3.7 percent in the second quarter of 1991, allowing only a 4-percent average for the first half of the year.\n569-\n570-17\n571-\n572-FEDERAL FISCAL OPERATIONS\n573-","stderr":"","exit_code":0},"isError":false}}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
